// Pull request: #6308 Add OpenSSH PKCS11Provider DLL load detection
// Pull request URL: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/pull/6308
// Pull request author: Diablo-research
// Change: new rule
// Title: Potential DLL Execution Via OpenSSH PKCS11Provider
// Sigma ID: 28869694-2516-46e0-86ca-a90478e50731
// Status: experimental | Level: medium
// Author: Nazar Diachok (@Diablo-research)
// Date: 2026-09-19 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: stealth, T1202
// Description: Detects the use of the OpenSSH PKCS11Provider option with SSH or SCP. This option can be abused
//   to load a DLL from a local path or remote SMB share into a trusted OpenSSH process.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate use of smart cards, hardware security modules, or other PKCS#11 providers with OpenSSH
// References:
//   - https://lolbas-project.github.io/lolbas/Binaries/Ssh/
//   - https://lolbas-project.github.io/lolbas/Binaries/Scp/
//   - https://man.openbsd.org/ssh_config#PKCS11Provider
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/18bbb0bda879c1cd0db43380cb538bc405e63ff3/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_openssh_pkcs11provider.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath endswith "\\ssh.exe" or FolderPath endswith "\\scp.exe") and ProcessCommandLine contains "PKCS11Provider="
